  Expired --> [*]: handshakes fail`,caption:"The dangerous edge is Renewed to Stale: the certificate on disk is fresh but the process is still serving the old one. Automate the reload, and alert when a renewed certificate has not been picked up."}),(0,n.jsx)("div",{className:"grid gap-4 lg:grid-cols-3 mt-4",children:lz.map(e=>(0,n.jsxs)("div",{className:"rounded-lg border border-border bg-muted/30 p-4",children:[(0,n.jsx)("div",{className:"font-medium text-foreground mb-2",children:e.title}),(0,n.jsx)("p",{className:"text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-6",children:e.body})]},e.title))})]}),(0,n.jsxs)("article",{className:"rounded-xl border border-border bg-card p-5",children:[(0,n.jsx)("h4",{className:"font-semibold text-foreground mb-3",children:"The HTTP defaults that make a TLS boundary actually secure"}),(0,n.jsx)("p",{className:"text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-6 mb-4",children:"A correct TLS handshake protects bytes in transit, but it does not by itself stop a session cookie from leaking, a cross-origin page from reading your responses, or a browser from quietly falling back to plaintext. Those are HTTP-level concerns, and each one is a small policy you set deliberately. The five below are the defaults worth treating as non-negotiable on a public boundary, with exceptions made consciously rather than by omission."}),(0,n.jsx)("div",{className:"grid gap-4 lg:grid-cols-3",children:lD.map(e=>(0,n.jsxs)("div",{className:"rounded-lg border border-border bg-muted/30 p-4",children:[(0,n.jsx)("div",{className:"font-medium text-foreground mb-2",children:e.title}),(0,n.jsx)("p",{className:"text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-6",children:e.body})]},e.title))})]}),(0,n.jsxs)("article",{className:"rounded-xl border border-border bg-card p-5",children:[(0,n.jsx)("h4",{className:"font-semibold text-foreground mb-3",children:"Debugging TLS failures without weakening security"}),(0,n.jsx)("p",{className:"text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-6 mb-4",children:"TLS fail