aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu // full std userspace`})})]})]}),(0,n.jsxs)("section",{className:"space-y-4",children:[(0,n.jsxs)("div",{className:"flex items-center gap-2",children:[(0,n.jsx)(tQ,{className:"h-5 w-5 text-primary"}),(0,n.jsx)("h3",{className:"text-lg font-semibold text-foreground",children:"Mental model"})]}),(0,n.jsx)("p",{className:"text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-6",children:"Three ideas do most of the work in this chapter, and getting them straight up front prevents nearly all of the confusion newcomers have with no_std. The first is that you are not learning a different language. The second is that no_std and no-heap are separate decisions. The third is that the target triple, not your preference, sets the ceiling on what you may use."}),(0,n.jsx)("div",{className:"grid gap-4 lg:grid-cols-3",children:mh.map(e=>(0,n.jsxs)("div",{className:"rounded-lg border border-border bg-card p-4",children:[(0,n.jsx)("h4",{className:"font-semibold text-foreground mb-2",children:e.title}),(0,n.jsx)("p",{className:"text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-6",children:e.body})]},e.title))})]}),(0,n.jsxs)("section",{className:"space-y-5",children:[(0,n.jsxs)("div",{className:"flex items-center gap-2",children:[(0,n.jsx)(tK,{className:"h-5 w-5 text-primary"}),(0,n.jsx)("h3",{className:"text-lg font-semibold text-foreground",children:"Core concepts"})]}),(0,n.jsx)("p",{className:"text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-6",children:"With the three-ring picture in place, the rest of the chapter walks the concrete decisions a senior engineer makes: how to lay out a crate so each function lives at the right ring, when no_std is the right call at all, how to manage memory without a heap, how ownership changes when DMA and registers enter the picture, how the embedded ecosystem is layered, what happens at FFI and wasm boundaries, and how to test code that may never run on your laptop. Each subsection pairs the reasoning with a small, runnable shape."}),(0,n.jsxs)("article",{className:"rounded-xl border border-border bg-card p-5",children:[(0,n.jsx)("h4",{className:"font-semibold text-foreground mb-3",children:"The three surfaces in practice: core, alloc, std, and what the target triple decides"}),(0,n.jsx)("p",{className:"text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-6 mb-4",children:"The standard library you reach for on a server is really three layers stacked on top of each other, and no_std lets you opt out of the upper ones. Knowing exactly what lives in each layer is what lets you place a function at the lowest ring it can tolerate. The cards below name the contents of each surface; the code that follows shows how one crate routes its public API across all three at once."}),(0,n.jsx)("div",{className:"grid gap-4 lg:grid-cols-3",children:mu.map(e=>(0,n.jsxs)("div",{className:"rounded-lg border border-border bg-muted/30 p-4",children:[(0,n.jsx)("div",{className:"font-medium text-foreground mb-2",children:e.title}),(0,n.jsx)("p",{className:"text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-6",children:e.body})]},e.title))}),(0,n.jsxs)("div",{className:"mt-5",children:[(0,n.jsx)("h5",{className:"font-medium text-foreground mb-2",children:"The crate-root pattern, and how to read it"}),(0,n.jsxs)("p",{className:"text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-6",children:["Look at the listing below as three doors, one per surface. The crate is no_std unless the caller turns on the ",(0,n.jsx)("code",{className:"px-1 py-0.5 rounded bg-muted font-mono text-[11px]",children:"std"})," feature. ",(0,n.jsx)("code",{className:"px-1 py-0.5 rounded bg-muted font-mono text-[11px]",children:"checksum"})," takes only a slice and so compiles everywhere. ",(0,n.jsx)("code",{className:"px-1 py-0.5 rounded bg-muted font-mono text-[11px]",children:"encode_frame"})," returns an owned ",(0,n.jsx)("code",{className:"px-1 py-0.5 rounded bg-muted font-mono text-[11px]",children:"Vec<u8>"})," and is therefore behind the alloc door. ",(0,n.jsx)("code",{className:"px-1 py-0.5 rounded bg-muted font-mono text-[11px]",children:"write_diagnostic"})," formats a host-facing diagnostic string for an environment with an OS and lives behind the std door. A firmware build enables nothing extra and sees only the first function; a server build enables std and sees all three. The diagram traces those three paths before you read the code."]}),(0,n.jsx)("div",{className:"mt-3",children:(0,n.jsx)(r5,{chart:`flowchart TD
